  il/ofek-hadash-2008 — Israeli collective agreements between the State of
  Israel and the Teachers' Union of Israel, decisions of their joint follow-up
  committee, circulars of the Ministry of Education and of the Wage
  Commissioner, and Chapter 9 of the Law for the Achievement of Budget
  Objectives ... 5785-2025. The applicable source terms are UNDETERMINED, and
  this row is the first here to be undetermined on TWO independent questions
  rather than one; see that row's SOURCE-LICENSE.md.

  The first question is the usual one, complicated by the material not being
  uniform: an Act published in Reshumot, a collective agreement filed under the
  Collective Agreements Law 5717-1957, and a ministry circular are three
  different cases, and Israel's Copyright Act 5768-2007 s 6 has not been
  checked against any of them here.

  The second question has no precedent in this repository. Unlike every other
  row, nothing was fetched from an official publisher. The encoding was read
  entirely from a third-party published corpus --
  https://github.com/morimovilimcatala/ofek-hadash-corpus -- which gathered,
  transcribed and re-encoded the instruments as Akoma Ntoso, and which CARRIES
  NO LICENCE FILE. A transcription is an act of authorship whatever the status
  of the thing transcribed, so permission to use that compilation has neither
  been granted nor established. The digests recorded in that row's
  registers/source-bundle.json are over that corpus's HTML, not over the
  ministry PDFs behind it.

  What the row reproduces is narrower than sg/penal-code-1871: it is NOT in
  inert style and carries no statutory prose inline. It reproduces two salary
  tables in full (648 cells, 224 of them distinct), the supplement percentages
  and shekel floors, short quoted phrases of a few words each, and one Hebrew
  string -- the payslip line name that section 17 of the 2026 agreement
  requires. Its document register also carries English titles and short
  summaries of all 282 corpus documents, which are this project's own
  translations but are derived from that corpus's text. All of that is material
  for whatever assessment settles the two questions. No attribution is recorded
  because none has yet been established, not because none is required -- and if
  the second question resolves in favour of the corpus's compiler, an
  attribution to that corpus belongs here.

  il/penal-law-1977 — the Penal Law 5737-1977 (חוק העונשין, תשל״ז–1977), an Act
  of the Knesset, sections 383, 384, 402, 403 and 404. The applicable source
  terms are UNDETERMINED, on the same TWO questions as il/ofek-hadash-2008 and
  for the same reason; see that subject's source/SOURCE-LICENSE.md, which sits
  beside source/ at the subject level rather than inside the encoding row.

  The first question is whether Israel's Copyright Law 5768-2007 s 6 places the
  statute outside copyright. That reading is the usual one but has not been
  checked against the section text here.

  The second is the consolidation. Nothing was fetched from Reshumot, which is
  geo-blocked outside Israel and publishes no consolidated text. The deposit is
  revision 3023424 of the Open Book of Laws page on Hebrew Wikisource, whose
  contributions are published under CC BY-SA 3.0. The merging of 155 amendments
  into one running text is the volunteers' and the bot's work, not the Knesset's,
  and s 6 says nothing about that layer.

  What this row reproduces is narrower than sg/penal-code-1871 and wider than
  il/ofek-hadash-2008: five sections are quoted VERBATIM in Hebrew, in comments
  above the rule that encodes each, and the operative phrases of those same five
  ride as inert strings inside the rules. That is roughly two hundred words of
  the Act. No other section is reproduced. The English renderings tagged beside
  every rule are this project's own translations and are not a translation of
  any published English version. No attribution is recorded because none has yet
  been established, not because none is required -- and if the second question
  resolves in favour of the Wikisource compilation, the attribution named in
  that subject's SOURCE-LICENSE.md belongs here.

  us/chubb-hospital-cash — the first row here whose terms are DETERMINED rather
  than undetermined, and therefore the first that records a real attribution
  rather than an open question. The synthetic policy encoded in this row is
  Appendix A, "ILLUSTRATION 1: SUPPLEMENTAL HOSPITALIZATON CASH POLICY", of:

      Oliver R. Goodenough & Preston J. Carlson, "Words or code first? Is the
      legacy document or a code statement the better starting point for
      complexity-reducing legal automation?" (2024). PMC10894687.
      https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10894687/

  That article is distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License
  (CC BY 4.0), http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ — verified against
  the PMC full text on 2026-08-31. The encodings quote the policy inline (inert
  style), so this attribution discharges a licence CONDITION and must not be
  removed.

  The text actually encoded is a modified variant printed at arXiv:2502.17638
  Appendix A.1, which carries arXiv's non-exclusive distribution licence and
  does not itself cite Goodenough & Carlson for the policy. We rely on the CC BY
  grant above, not on that paper. See the row's SOURCE-LICENSE.md for what that
  distinction does and does not settle.

